    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Most Tanzanians carry a Christian or Muslim first name, often with a Swahili name beside it, and take their father's first name as their surname, so a woman may be Neema Joseph or Mwanaisha Hassani. Many families now keep a fixed surname from a grandfather's name, like Mushi or Masanja. Swahili names given for the day or order of birth are used across the country: Juma (born on a Friday), Hamisi (Thursday), Tatu (third-born), and the twin names Kulwa and Doto. Chagga Christians in Kilimanjaro and Arusha favour names beginning with Eli- (God), like Elibariki and Eliaichi. Sukuma names often mark the season or events around a birth, like Kabula (born in the rains). Maasai and Arusha names carry the male prefix Le- or Lo- and the female Na-. Elders are greeted with Shikamoo whatever their name.",
